### 7. The Outline

---

**H2: The Two Numbers That Should Reset Your AI Strategy**
- McKinsey: 80% of tracked AI projects net negative to the business
- MIT: 95% of AI projects never exit pilot
- These are not indictments of AI as a technology -- they are indictments of how organizations are approaching it
- H3: What "net negative" actually means -- spend that wasn't overcome by value
- H3: What "never exits pilot" actually means -- built for demo, not for production

**H2: Tool-First Thinking Is the Root Cause**
- Board pressure produces the hammer-looking-for-nails pattern
- "We got to have AI. Got to do AI." -- the strategic failure mode Devlin sees repeatedly
- H3: The computer-centric workflow filter -- where AI actually applies vs. where it doesn't
- H3: If I make something I do once a quarter 10% faster, the math doesn't work
- Section note: This is where the villain is named explicitly. Don't soften it.

**H2: There Is No Company-Level AI Maturity**
- The most counterintuitive structural claim in the article -- worth a dedicated section
- Departmental maturity, not organizational maturity
- Different work, different tools, different timelines
- H3: The departmental audit -- mapping tasks by volume and precision required
- H3: Generic tooling vs. departmental tooling (the distinction Devlin calls "downtown roads vs. specialized buildings")
- H3: The 12-month roadmap and the built-in revisit cadence -- AI is not a static market
- `[INSERT DIAGRAM: Two-tier AI tooling map -- generic/shared tools (document search, chat) at center, department-specific tools (accounting/ERP, IT/dev tooling) radiating out from it]`

**H2: Boxes First, Arrows Later**
- The specific methodology Devlin uses with clients
- H3: What a box is -- an isolated task or workflow that can be improved independently
- H3: Why you ignore end-to-end automation at the start -- the complexity of arrows is where pilots die
- H3: Where the arrows matter eventually -- protection against over-reliance failure modes
- `[INSERT DIAGRAM: Simple boxes-and-arrows whiteboard sketch showing department workflow with boxes (tasks) and arrows (handoffs/automations), with the first-phase scope highlighted to show which arrows are out of scope initially]`

**H2: You Have More People Problems Than Technology Problems**
- The identity-attachment mechanism: "I am a software developer" vs. "I produce software"
- Why this matters: moving someone's cheese means threatening their professional identity
- H3: Two failure modes -- over-reliance and under-reliance
  - Over-reliance: treating AI as infallible, missing the new error types AI introduces (different from human errors), not training quality-check patterns to match
  - Under-reliance: hard-coding specifics, prompting every time instead of capturing learnings into agents, losing most of the efficiency gain
- H3: The saboteur dynamic -- forced adoption creates resistance that doesn't have to be intentional or malicious
- H3: The better sequence -- start by automating what people dislike anyway, then expand into what they do like
- `[INSERT DIAGRAM: Over-reliance / Under-reliance spectrum with failure modes on each end and the intervention strategy for each]`

**H2: The Hybrid Intelligence Advantage (Why It's Neither AI Nor Humans)**
- MIT Sloan Review 2024-2025 data: AI alone 25% failure rate on complex tasks, humans alone 15%, hybrid <5%
- This is not a transitional finding -- it is the design target
- H3: What "hybrid intelligence" requires -- humans must be able to catch the errors AI makes (which differ from human errors)
- H3: Why this reframes the workforce question from replacement to capability expansion

**H2: Leaders Use AI First**
- "Leaders eat last; leaders use AI first"
- The Improving internal model: share experiments, not success stories; require equal balance of failures and successes
- Executive team goes first -- publicly modeling failure establishes the permission structure
- H3: Your communication plan is part of your AI strategy, not an afterthought
- H3: What employees are actually hearing in the media -- the 1974 manufacturing-robots prediction as a historical grounding
- Section note: The host's expense report story works well here as a candid leadership vulnerability example -- model it explicitly.

**H2: The Token Economics Your Finance Team Isn't Watching**
- AI costs are heavily VC-subsidized right now -- prices are likely to increase
- The 130x cost differential between cheapest and most expensive models is not widely understood
- H3: Super users and the $500-$2,000/month additional cost reality -- material at scale
- H3: The exception detection mindset vs. the cost-obsession mindset -- per-token frugality is the wrong signal; order-of-magnitude anomalies are the right signal
- H3: Target ROI of 5-10x in year one as the governing cost benchmark -- if token costs are your main concern, you're probably tackling the wrong problem
- `[INSERT DIAGRAM: Model cost tier comparison (low/mid/premium) with appropriate use cases and approximate cost multipliers at each tier]`

**H2: The Case for Moving Faster Instead (Skeptic's View)**
- Counter-argument: Tool-first experimentation is how you discover problems you didn't know you had. Startups move fast this way -- why not enterprises?
- Counter-argument: Analysis paralysis is a real risk. A 12-month roadmap is a long time when AI is moving this fast.
- Devlin's response: Startups have no cultural anchors, no identity-attached workforces, no existing process debt. The problem-first approach is not slower for them because they don't have the change management burden. For enterprises, the cost of a failed pilot is not just the budget -- it's the credibility for the next initiative.
- The kitchen remodel vs. teardown frame: incremental departmental iteration is faster in practice than company-wide big-bang transformation because it doesn't trigger the saboteur dynamic.

